{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he shall not profane his seed among his people</b> — that is, if he married one of the disqualified women, his seed from her is profaned with respect to the sanctity of priesthood; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 21:15). The Rav explained it as referring to disqualification alone: \"these he shall not take\" — so that he not profane his seed. And according to our Sages (Kiddushin 78a), it is a second negative commandment, and thus they said: if he betrothed and cohabited, he receives two sets of lashes, one on account of \"he shall not take\" and one on account of \"he shall not profane.\" And they said: if he cohabited without betrothal, he receives lashes on account of \"he shall not profane.\" Now these are two negative commandments, and it was also derived as reasoning that if he betrothed but did not cohabit, he does not receive lashes — what is the reason? \"He shall not take,\" on account of \"he shall not profane.\" If so, its meaning is: \"these he shall not take,\" for I warn him by means of \"he shall not profane his seed.\" And from this we ourselves learn that the seed from the disqualified women is profaned."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:24.563231323Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}